In article <1993Apr28.180623.9776@math.utexas.edu> nzook@vendela.ma.utexas.edu (Nathan Zook) writes:
>As for spoilers "spoiling" it...
>
>The game practically FORCES you to use some spoilers--there are just too
>many things that would take too long to guess.  Consider the following,
>from the point of view of an "unspoiled" player:
>
>Arrive at Oracle, pay, Oracle says you can tell what rings are what by
>dumping down a drain. GREAT!  So just how do you tell?!?!?  Some are
>obvious, but, since you DON'T EVEN KNOW what rings are available, how
>are you to tell what a ring is by the effect?
>
>There are similar problems with armor, etc.

Well, this is sort of a stupid thing to say :-) - there are PLENTY of identify
scrolls around, and I have never had ANY problem finding one of almost
everything in an average of five (good) games... (good as in I get down
below 10th level or so)  I think that it is all part of the game; I did
not initially read any spoilers, and when I finally found them and
read them, I knew more than haalf of the info contained.  Of course,
there are some things you just have to try, but...

Something that might help (and that I'm not taking into account) is that
a neophyte may not have read a lot of fantasy... IMHO, it is sorta necessary
to get all of the gimmicks in nethack :-)
>
>I guess my point is that the game is complicated enough that it needs
>SIZABLE documentation.  Since this documentation does not exsist, the spoiler
>files are the only things left.

Well, it just makes it more realistic!  As realistic as any fantasy
game is, at any rate...  I never even thought that in any of the books
I read about adventures, etc. that there would be a huge guidebook
to the mines of wherever they were going.
>
